Nodes & Edges
        Elements representing entities (states) and their connections (relationships)
        LC 1192

Node Traversal
        Track visited physical locations or states; once a node is marked visited, 
        it is skipped entirely because returning to it yields no new utility
        LC 864

Edge Traversal
        Track visited connections or transitions; you can revisit the same node multiple times as long as you 
        arrive via a completely fresh, unused path
        LC 332
        
Traversal
        Systematically visiting every reachable entity in the network to inspect or catalog states.
        LC 847
Cyclic
        Graphs containing paths that start and end at the same node, necessitating a visited set to avoid infinite loops
        LC 261
Dense
        A graph where the number of edges approaches the maximum possible ($E \approx V^2$), 
        making adjacency matrices better than adjacency lists
        LC 1135

BFS
        Explore layer-by-layer using a queue to find the shortest path in unweighted graphs
        LC 815

DFS
        Explore as deep as possible along each branch using recursion or a stack before backtracking
        LC 827

Matrix BFS
        Standard layer-by-layer traversal adapted to a grid using directional coordinate arrays to step through valid neighbor cells.
        LC 1293

Matrix DFS
        Deep grid-exploration often paired with backtracking to find paths or count distinct component properties.
        LC 329

Union Find
        Efficiently group elements into sets using find (with path compression) and union (by rank) 
        to check dynamic connectivity or detect cycles in $O(\alpha(N))$ time.
        LC 952

Connectivity
        General reachability evaluation between structural components or isolated network sub-graphs
        LC 1697

Euler / Hierholzer's Algorithm
        Find a path or circuit that visits every single edge exactly once by using DFS modified to post-order trace stuck paths 
        and splice them together.
        LC 332

Weighted
        Edges carry scalar cost values (distance, time, cost) changing search from hop-count to cost optimization.
        LC 1976

Shortest Path
        The optimal traversal trajectory maximizing or minimizing a target edge weight metric between two nodes
        LC 1368

Dijkstra
        Use a min-priority queue to greedily extract the minimum distance node to find the shortest path from a single source in 
        non-negative weighted graphs.
        LC 778

Multi-Source Dijkstra
        Initialize the min-priority queue with multiple starting nodes simultaneously to find the shortest distance 
        from any source to all destinations.
        LC 2050

Bellman Ford
        Relax all edges $V-1$ times to find shortest paths from a single source, capable of handling negative weights 
        and detecting negative cycles
        LC 787

Floyd Warshall
        Use dynamic programming ($O(V^3)$) to calculate the shortest path between all pairs of nodes by checking 
        if an intermediate node $K$ offers a shorter route.
        LC 1334

Tarjan
        LC 1192

Directed
        Edges possess orientation; traveling from Node A to Node B does not automatically allow travel from B back to A
        LC 207

Topological Ordering
        Linear ordering of vertices in a DAG such that for every directed edge $uv$, node $u$ comes before $v$.
        LC 1857

Kahn's Algorithm
        Find a topological ordering in a DAG by iteratively removing nodes with an in-degree of 0 using a queue
        LC 269

Tree
        A highly restricted connected, undirected graph with $N$ nodes and exactly $N-1$ edges containing zero cycles.
        LC 124
MST
        A subset of edges connecting all vertices together with the absolute lowest collective edge weight sum without cycles.
        LC 1631

Prim's Algorithm
        Build a Minimum Spanning Tree greedily by adding the cheapest possible edge that connects any node 
        inside the growing tree to a node outside it
        LC 1584

Kruskal's Algorithm
        Build a Minimum Spanning Tree by sorting all edges by weight and greedily adding them using Union-Find to 
        skip edges that create cycles
        LC 1489

Euler Ordering
        Flattening a tree into a linear array via its DFS entry and exit timestamps to resolve subtree queries with 
        simple array ranges
        LC 2322

Tree DP
        Execute dynamic programming over tree structures using bottom-up DFS to compute optimal sub-solutions from 
        child sub-trees up to parent nodes
        LC 834
